            .case-study__intro-text-wrapper
                .columns
                    .column
                        %p.case-study__introduction
                            Island Finance has spent over 65 years as one of Puerto Rico's leading personal loan providers, with 49 branches across the island. This redesign brought that legacy into a digital experience where borrowers understand exactly what they're signing up for from the start, with simple, self-service ways to manage their loan and access their information after signing.
                    .column
                        %p.case-study__introduction
                            I redesigned Island Finance's website to bring clarity into a lending experience that had grown outdated and hard to navigate. I led research, UX strategy, and design across the full borrower journey, focused on making every step feel straightforward and easy to follow.
        .case-study__problem
            .container
                .section-heading__wrapper
                    .columns.is-vcentered.is-variable.is-1.is-mobile
                        .column.is-narrow
                            = inline_svg('search.svg')
                        .column
                            %h2 What wasn't working?
                .problem__pill-wrapper
                    .columns.is-mobile.is-multiline.is-variable.is-1
                        .column.is-narrow
                            .pill.blue-3
                                %p Hidden Fees
                        .column.is-narrow
                            .pill.blue-2
                                %p Confusing account changes
                        .column.is-narrow
                            .pill.blue-3
                                %p Billing mistakes borrowers pay for
                        .column.is-narrow
                            .pill.blue-2
                                %p Branch Dependency
                .section__statement-wrapper
                    %p Island Finance's old website made it easy to misunderstand your own loan. Fees were buried. Changing a payment date could quietly affect what someone owed, with no clear explanation of the impact. And because managing a loan meant calling in or visiting a branch, even small mistakes on Island Finance's end, like a phone payment processed a few cents short, could leave a borrower on the hook for interest they never should have owed.
                
        .case-study__solution-wrapper
            .container
                .section-heading__wrapper
                    .columns.is-vcentered.is-variable.is-1.is-mobile
                        .column.is-narrow
                            = inline_svg('search.svg')
                        .column
                            %h2 How did we solve it?
                .section__statement-wrapper
                    %p Every decision here got checked against three things: could a borrower see the real cost before committing to anything, could they fix a problem themselves without needing to call in, and could the flow survive someone doing it on a phone, distracted, mid-errand. 
                .columns.is-vcentered.is-reversed.is-mobile.is-multiline
                    .column.is-4-tablet.is-12-mobile
                        .solution__text-wrapper
                            %h3 Fees Upfront
                            %p Rather than revealing rates after someone finished an application, the calculator shows aproximate rates and payments upfront, before a borrower commits to anything. We also expanded Island Finance's blog with plain-language guides on APR, fees, and credit, so borrowers encountering these terms for the first time had somewhere to actually understand what the calculator was showing them.
                    .column.is-8-tablet.is-12-mobile
                .columns.is-vcentered.is-mobile.is-multiline
                    .column.is-8-tablet.is-12-mobile
                    .column.is-4-tablet.is-12-mobile
                        .solution__text-wrapper
                            %h3 Confirmation at key moments
                            %p Borrowers get a notification when they finish an application and when a payment goes through.
                .columns.is-vcentered.is-reversed.is-mobile.is-multiline
                    .column.is-4-tablet.is-12-mobile
                        .solution__text-wrapper
                            %h3 Self-service account management
                            %p Borrowers can now make payments online and pick up a saved application right where they left off. Full account-based management is planned for the next phase.
                    .column.is-8-tablet.is-12-mobile
                .columns.is-vcentered.is-mobile.is-multiline
                    .column.is-8-tablet.is-12-mobile
                    .column.is-4-tablet.is-12-mobile
                        .solution__text-wrapper
                            %h3 Clear impact of every change
                            %p Changing a payment date is meant to show a borrower exactly what that change costs before they confirm it. This is proposed for the next phase alongside the dashboard.
        .case-study__impact-wrapper
            .container
                .section-heading__wrapper
                    .columns.is-vcentered.is-variable.is-1.is-mobile
                        .column.is-narrow
                            = inline_svg('search.svg')
                        .column
                            %h2 What did we accomplish?
                .quote__wrapper
                    .columns.is-is-vcentered.is-mobile.is-variable.is-1
                        .column.is-narrow
                            = inline_svg('comment.svg')
                        .column
                            %p From a customer review, before the redesign, reworded for privacy
                    .quote__text-wrapper
                        %p "Was charged a late fee after a payment was processed a few cents short, an error made by the cashier, not the customer."
                .impact__card-wrapper
                    .columns
                        .column
                            .impact-bullet
                                .pill.blue-3
                                    %p Trasparency
                                %h3 Every Fee Is Shown Upfront
                                %p before a borrower even applies
                        .column
                            .impact-bullet
                                .pill.blue-2
                                    %p Paying a loan
                                %h3 Paying a loan takes one click
                                %p and no longer require a phone call or a branch visit
                        .column
                            .impact-bullet
                                .pill.yellow-2
                                    %p Confidence
                                %h3 A blog that explains fees
                                %p plain-language guides on fees, APR, and credit

        .case-study__research
            .container
                .columns
                    .column.is-5
                        .section-heading__wrapper
                            .columns.is-vcentered.is-variable.is-1.is-mobile
                                .column.is-narrow
                                    = inline_svg('search.svg')
                                .column
                                    %h2 What did the research tell us?
                    .column.is-7
                        .intro__wrapper
                            %p Three findings shaped every decision that followed, from what to show first to how much control to hand borrowers.
                        .research__card-wrapper
                            .research__card.red-4
                                = inline_svg('user-x.svg')
                                %h3 Drop Off
                                %p Most personal loan applicants abandon before finishing, with rates as high as 67% industry-wide
                                .card__solution-wrapper
                                    .columns.is-variable.is-1.is-mobile
                                        .column.is-narrow
                                            = inline_svg('arrow-right.svg')
                                        .column
                                            %p
                                                We 
                                                %span surfaced fees early, expanded the blog
                                                to build trust, and let people 
                                                %span save their application
                                                and return
                            .research__card.yellow-4
                                = inline_svg('device.svg')
                                %h3 Preference
                                %p 55% of bank customers manage accounts primarily through a mobile app, versus 8% who prefer a branch
                                .card__solution-wrapper
                                    .columns.is-variable.is-1.is-mobile
                                        .column.is-narrow
                                            = inline_svg('arrow-right.svg')
                                        .column
                                            %p
                                                We 
                                                %span moved payments online,
                                                with full self-service account management planned next
                            .research__card.green-4
                                = inline_svg('scale.svg')
                                %h3 The gap
                                %p Digital-first lenders had already made upfront APR and calculators standard, a bar local competitors hadn't met
                                .card__solution-wrapper
                                    .columns.is-variable.is-1.is-mobile
                                        .column.is-narrow
                                            = inline_svg('arrow-right.svg')
                                        .column
                                            %p
                                                We
                                                %span rebuilt the calculator
                                                to show all fees upfront
            
        
        .case-study__gallery
            .container
                .section-heading__wrapper
                    %h2 Introducing Island Finance
                .gallery__home        
                    .gallery__home-img-wrapper
                        =image_tag "https://nicolada-website.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/Island+Finance+Settings.webp", :loading => 'lazy', :width => 1344, :height => 560, :class => "home-img"
                    %h3 Homepage
                    %p The homepage had one job: get a skeptical borrower to trust this enough to keep going. Instead of leading with a sales pitch, it opens with the exact loan range and a way to start estimating a payment immediately, so the first thing a visitor does is get real numbers.
                .gallery-section.gallery__calculator
                    .columns.is-vcentered.is-mobile.is-multiline
                        .column.is-8-tablet.is-12-mobile
                        .column.is-4-tablet.is-12-mobile
                            %h3 Loan calculator
                            %p This is where the "transparent rates as baseline" gap gets closed directly. Every fee and rate shows before a borrower commits to anything, and checking a rate doesn't affect their credit. Someone can walk away at this stage with real numbers and no risk, which is exactly what the research said borrowers needed before they'd trust a lender enough to apply.
                .gallery-section.gallery__application
                    .columns.is-reversed.is-vcentered.is-mobile.is-multiline
                        .column.is-4-tablet.is-12-mobile
                            %h3 Application
                            %p Since two out of three loan applicants abandon before finishing industry-wide, every unnecessary step in the flow was a place to lose someone. The application was simplified into fewer, clearer steps, and progress now saves automatically as a borrower moves through it, so they don't need to start over.
                        .column.is-8-tablet.is-12-mobile
                .gallery-section.gallery__dashboard
                    .columns.is-vcentered.is-mobile.is-multiline
                        .column.is-8-tablet.is-12-mobile
                        .column.is-4-tablet.is-12-mobile
                            %h3 Dashboard
                            %p This was designed to solve a real, documented problem, managing a loan by phone or branch visit was inconvenient, and in one case, exactly where a billing error happened. It didn't make it into this launch; the platform's codebase needs updates to support it first. In the meantime, moving payments online and surfacing fees earlier in the calculator addressed part of what the dashboard was meant to solve, with the fuller fix planned for a future phase.
                .gallery-section.gallery__payments
                    .columns.is-reversed.is-vcentered.is-mobile.is-multiline
                        .column.is-4-tablet.is-12-mobile
                            %h3 Payments & settings
                            %p Confirmation notifications for finishing an application and completing a payment mean a borrower never has to wonder whether something went through. Full due-date reminders and account-based alerts are part of what the dashboard phase would add.
                        .column.is-8-tablet.is-12-mobile
                .gallery-section.gallery__blog
                    .columns.is-vcentered.is-mobile.is-multiline
                        .column.is-8-tablet.is-12-mobile
                        .column.is-4-tablet.is-12-mobile
                            %h3 Blog
                            %p Many of Island Finance's borrowers are people traditional banks had turned away, often without much prior exposure to how loans, APR, or credit actually work. Reaching underserved communities with financial literacy content is a growing focus across the lending industry, and Island Finance's own mission put that responsibility directly on this redesign. Expanding the blog gave borrowers a place to actually understand fees and terms before they had to ask.
        .case-study__takeaways
            .container
                .section-heading__wrapper
                    .columns.is-vcentered.is-variable.is-1.is-mobile
                        .column.is-narrow
                            = inline_svg('search.svg')
                        .column
                            %h2 What did I learn along the way?
                .takeaways__wrapper
                    .columns
                        .column
                            %h3 Trust-building doesn't have to wait for the full system
                            %p The dashboard and full account creation were core to the original design, but the platform's outdated codebase and the project's timeline meant they couldn't ship in this phase. Rather than let that mean the underlying problems went unsolved, we found smaller ways to address the same needs: payments moved online, applications save progress automatically, and the calculator surfaces fees earlier in the process. None of these fully replace what the dashboard was meant to do, but borrowers weren't left with the original problem while that work is planned for a future phase.
                        .column
                            %h3 Legacy infrastructure is a design constraint
                            %p I came into this project focused on the borrower experience. What I learned is that a redesign is only as good as the codebase it has to live in, some of the highest-value fixes here got blocked not by the design, but by what the platform could actually support. I'd scope future phases with that reality earlier, rather than designing the ideal first and negotiating it down later.
                %p This project is ongoing, and the dashboard remains the highest priority for the next phase, it's the piece most directly tied to preventing the kind of billing error that had already cost a real borrower money.
        .case-study__link
            .container
                .columns.is-vcentered
                    .column
                    .column
                        %h3 Want the full picture?
                        %p This is the story at a glance, the decisions, the research, and what shipped. The full case study goes deeper: the complete competitive audit, every persona, and the full screen-by-screen walkthrough.
                        .btn__wrapper
                            = link_to "View Full Case Study", "/ux-ui/island-finance-web-full", :class => "btn"
